---
layout: base.njk
pagination:
  data: sites.entries
  size: 1
  alias: entry
permalink: "/site/{{ entry.slug }}/"
eleventyComputed:
  title: "{{ entry.displayUrl }}"
  # The measured site's own mark, so a tab full of these is readable by icon
  # rather than by a truncated domain. base.njk falls back to the project's
  # favicon wherever a page does not set one.
  pageFavicon: "{{ entry.url | avatar(meta.avatarService) }}"
---

<article class="detail">

{#
	One screenshot per site page, from 11ty's service. Deliberately not on the
	leaderboards: those run to a thousand rows, and a thousand screenshot
	requests is not a page, it is a load test pointed at someone else's server.

	Not our own Lighthouse capture, though the Filmstrip section below is exactly
	that. Those frames are thumbnails sized to the emulated viewport — 250px wide
	on a mobile run — which is right for a strip of eight and too small for the
	one picture at the top of the page.

	Only above a performance score of 75, and only once there is a measurement.
	The service renders the page on a timer, so the slower the site the more
	likely the capture is of a half-drawn or empty page — a picture of our own
	screenshot timeout rather than of the site. Below the threshold there is
	nothing worth showing and a request not worth making.

	Lazy, and sized in the markup so the text beside it does not jump when the
	image lands. Decorative rather than described: what the site looks like is
	not information this page is making a claim about, and "screenshot of
	example.com" read aloud tells you nothing you did not just hear from the
	heading above it.
#}
{% set SCREENSHOT_MIN_PERFORMANCE = 75 %}
{% set screenshot = "" %}
{#
	Nothing at all when the page we measured was a bot check. The screenshot
	service is as automated as we are, so it meets the same waiting room — and a
	picture of Cloudflare captioned with this site's name claims we saw something
	we did not.
#}
{% set shotWidth = 650 %}
{% set shotHeight = 650 %}
{#
	Our own capture first, where we have one: the picture our own Chrome saw,
	taken on the same page load as the numbers beside it. Every site gets one
	from the axe pass now, so the service below is only for sites that have not
	come round for measurement since — see lib/axe.js.

	No performance floor on this branch: that floor exists because the service
	renders on a timer and photographs half-drawn pages, and our capture is a
	byproduct of a measurement that already finished.
#}
{% if not entry.interstitial and entry.ownScreenshot %}
	{% set screenshot = entry.ownScreenshot.src %}
	{% set shotWidth = entry.ownScreenshot.width or 650 %}
	{% set shotHeight = entry.ownScreenshot.height or 650 %}
{% elif not entry.interstitial and entry.latest.lab.scores.performance >= SCREENSHOT_MIN_PERFORMANCE %}
	{% set screenshot = entry.url | screenshot(meta.screenshotService) %}
{% endif %}

<header class="detail-head{{ ' has-shot' if screenshot }}">
	<div class="detail-head-text">
	{#
		The site's own mark beside its name — the same image the tab shows, so the
		page and the tab agree about what you are looking at.

		Decorative: the domain is right next to it, and "favicon for example.com"
		read aloud adds nothing to a heading that already says example.com. Sized
		in the markup as well as in CSS so the heading does not reflow when the
		image lands, and lazy would be wrong for something above the fold.
	#}
	<h2 class="page-hed">
		{%- set siteIcon = entry.url | avatar(meta.avatarService) -%}
		{%- if siteIcon %}<img class="page-hed-mark" src="{{ siteIcon }}" width="32" height="32" alt="" decoding="async">{% endif -%}
		{{ entry.displayUrl }}
	</h2>
	<p class="detail-url"><a href="{{ entry.url }}">{{ entry.url }}</a></p>
	{#
		The same six rings the leaderboard shows for this row, so arriving from a
		table lands on the figures you clicked. Only the header summary — the
		Scores section below carries the trends and deltas, which is what you came
		to a detail page for.
	#}
	{% if entry.latest %}
	<div class="detail-rings">
		<span class="rings">
			{% for score in metrics.SCORES %}
			{% scoreRing entry.latest.lab.scores[score.key], score.label %}
			{% endfor %}
			{% axeRing entry.axe %}
			{% cwvRing entry.cwvFailures, entry.cwv.assessed %}
		</span>
	</div>
	{% endif %}
	<p class="data-age {{ 'stale-age' if entry.dataStale }}">
		{% if entry.latest %}
			{#- No dash and no full stop: the age is a pill rather than the end of a
			    sentence, and punctuation either side of it reads as stray marks. #}
			Data from {{ entry.latest.date | date("long") }}
			<span class="age-pill{{ ' stale' if entry.dataStale }}"><time datetime="{{ entry.latest.date | iso }}">{{ entry.latest.date | since }}</time> old</span>
			{%- if entry.dataStale %}, older than this category's {{ entry.staleAfterHours | duration }} freshness target{% endif %}
		{% else %}
			No successful measurement yet.
		{% endif %}
	</p>
	<p class="muted">
		{#
			Where this site currently places inside each category it belongs to —
			which is not its global rank, and is usually a much better position:
			a site can be 61st of 1,134 in one list and 1st of 19 in another.

			Only for categories that present as rankings. 11ty Emeritus sets
			`ranked: false` precisely so nobody reads a placing into it, and
			printing one here would say the thing that page refuses to.
		#}
		{#
			The unified rank first, before the per-category ones — the same board
			the home page shows, across every measured site. Plain text, unlike the
			category names beside it: those lead somewhere that lists the site,
			and this one has no page of its own to point at.

			Only for the top half. "Better than 12% of sites" is a true sentence
			that nobody put their site on this list to read, and the six rings
			above already say plainly how it is doing — this line exists to give
			credit, not to rank the bottom of the board to its face.
		#}
		{% if entry.ranks.overall %}
		Overall
		<span class="cat-rank" title="Ranked {{ entry.ranks.overall | num }} of {{ sites.stats.measured | num }} measured sites overall">#{{ entry.ranks.overall | num }}</span>
		{% endif %}
		{% for g in entry.groups %}
		{% set grp = sites.groups | groupById(g) %}
		<a href="/group/{{ g }}/">{{ entry.groupNames[loop.index0] }}</a>
		{%- if grp.ranked and entry.groupRanks[g].overall %}
		<span class="cat-rank" title="Ranked {{ entry.groupRanks[g].overall | num }} of {{ grp.measured | num }} measured sites in {{ entry.groupNames[loop.index0] }}">#{{ entry.groupRanks[g].overall | num }}</span>
		{#- No separator between them: each name is followed by its own rank pill,
		    and a comma after the pill reads as punctuation belonging to the
		    number rather than to the list. #}
		{%- endif %}
		{% endfor %} ·
		{{ entry.totalCount }} measurement{{ "s" if entry.totalCount != 1 }}
		{% if entry.windowed %}<span title="The report charts the most recent {{ entry.historyCount }}; the rest stay on disk">(charting last {{ entry.historyCount }})</span>{% endif %}
		{% if entry.firstMeasured %}· since {{ entry.firstMeasured | date }}{% endif %}
	</p>
	</div>
	{#
		After the text, not before it: the heading is what the page is about and
		should come first to a screen reader, and this way the source order and
		the stacked mobile order are the same thing.
	#}
	{% if screenshot %}
	{#
		Hidden from assistive technology and out of the tab order: this is the
		same destination as the URL linked in text directly beside it, and a
		second link to it would be a duplicate to read past and a duplicate to
		tab through. Removing it costs a screen reader nothing — the link is
		still there, in words.
	#}
	<a class="detail-shot-link" href="{{ entry.url }}" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
		<img class="detail-shot" src="{{ screenshot }}" width="{{ shotWidth }}" height="{{ shotHeight }}"
			alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
	</a>
	{% endif %}
</header>

{% if entry.stale and entry.latest %}
<div class="notice notice-warn">
	<strong>This data is <time datetime="{{ entry.latest.date | iso }}">{{ entry.latest.date | since }}</time> old.</strong>
	<p>
		Sites are measured on a rolling schedule, so figures here are the newest
		successful measurement rather than a reading taken just now. Comparisons
		against other sites may span different points in time.
	</p>
</div>
{% endif %}

{% if entry.redirectTo %}
<div class="notice notice-warn">
	<strong>This URL redirects to <code>{{ entry.redirectTo }}</code>.</strong>
	<p>
		Lighthouse follows the redirect, so the numbers below describe the
		<em>destination</em> page, not this one.
		{% if entry.redirectConfirmed %}
		The move is confirmed, so measurement has already followed it — no config
		change needed, and the history came along.
		{% elif entry.redirectReason == "temporary" %}
		The redirect is temporary (302/303/307), so it is treated as a detour
		rather than a move and history stays here.
		{% elif entry.redirectReason == "unstable" %}
		The destination has not been consistent across recent runs — that pattern
		usually means an A/B test or a geo split rather than a move.
		{% else %}
		Not yet confirmed as a move; it needs to lead to the same place on
		several consecutive runs first.
		{% endif %}
	</p>
</div>
{% endif %}

{% if entry.canonicalUrl and not entry.redirectTo %}
<div class="notice notice-info">
	<strong>This URL redirects to <code>{{ entry.canonicalUrl }}</code>.</strong>
	<p>
		A trailing slash only, so both forms are treated as one site rather than
		two — one history, measured once. Lighthouse follows the redirect, so the
		numbers below describe the canonical page; the only cost is an extra hop
		on each run.
	</p>
</div>
{% endif %}

{% if entry.movedFrom %}
<div class="notice notice-info">
	<strong>Moved from <code>{{ entry.movedFrom }}</code>.</strong>
	<p>
		History from the previous URL is included below{% if entry.moveAt %}, with the
		move on {{ entry.moveAt | date }}{% endif %}. A step-change around that point is
		likely the change of address rather than a regression.
	</p>
</div>
{% endif %}

{% if entry.currentlyFailing %}
<div class="notice notice-error">
	<strong>
		The most recent attempt failed{% if entry.consecutiveFailures > 1 %} ({{ entry.consecutiveFailures }} in a row){% endif %}.
	</strong>
	<code>{{ entry.lastError }}</code>
	{% if entry.latest %}
	<p>Numbers below are from the last successful run, <time datetime="{{ entry.latest.date | iso }}">{{ entry.latest.date | since }}</time> old.</p>
	{% else %}
	<p>There is no successful measurement for this site yet.</p>
	{% endif %}
	{% if entry.consecutiveFailures > 2 %}
	<p class="small">Retries are backing off, so this site is being attempted less often than the rest.</p>
	{% endif %}
</div>
{% endif %}

{% if entry.environmentDrift and entry.environmentDrift.suspect %}
<div class="notice notice-warn">
	{#
		"Than usual" would be a claim about this site's own past. The baseline is
		the median across every measurement in the project, so the comparison is
		against the fleet — said plainly, because a reader who assumes the wrong
		baseline draws the wrong conclusion from the same number.
	#}
	<strong>
		The measuring machine was {{ entry.environmentDrift.direction }} than
		{% if entry.environmentDrift.scope == "fleet" %}most in this project{% else %}usual for this site{% endif %}.
	</strong>
	<p>
		Lighthouse benchmark index was {{ entry.environmentDrift.current | num }},
		{#
			The percentage was parenthesised in an earlier version, and the reason
			survives the rewrite: a filter binds tighter than the multiplication in
			Nunjucks, so `ratio * 100 | round` rounded 100 and left the product
			alone — printing "34.300000000000004%".
		#}
		{{ (entry.environmentDrift.ratio * 100) | round | abs }}%
		{{ entry.environmentDrift.direction }} than
		{% if entry.environmentDrift.scope == "fleet" %}
			this project's median of {{ entry.environmentDrift.baseline | num }}.
		{% else %}
			this site's baseline of {{ entry.environmentDrift.baseline | num }}.
		{% endif %}
		CPU-bound metrics — TBT, TTI,
		main thread work — are affected by this and may have moved without the site
		changing at all.
	</p>
</div>
{% endif %}

{% if not entry.latest %}
<div class="empty"><p>No successful measurements recorded for this site yet.</p></div>
{% else %}

{#
	Built from the same conditions the sections use, held in variables so each
	one is written once. A hardcoded list would link to Field data on the nine
	sites in ten that CrUX has never heard of — a table of contents whose entries
	sometimes go nowhere is worse than none.

	Only rendered for a measured site: the branch above is the whole page
	otherwise.
#}
{% set hasStack = entry.generator or entry.host %}
{% set hasCwv = entry.cwv %}
{% set hasField = entry.latest.field %}
{% set hasFieldHistory = entry.fieldHistory and entry.fieldHistory.series.length %}
{#
	One picture is a screenshot, not a filmstrip. A page that painted before the
	first sample repeats that one frame for the rest of the strip, and the
	section would be a heading over the same image eight times.
#}
{% set hasFilmstrip = entry.filmstrip and entry.filmstrip.distinct > 1 and not entry.interstitial %}

{% set toc = [] %}
{% if hasStack %}{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "stack", label: "Stack" }), toc) %}{% endif %}
{% if hasCwv %}{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "cwv", label: "Core Web Vitals" }), toc) %}{% endif %}
{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "scores", label: "Scores" }), toc) %}
{% if hasFilmstrip %}{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "filmstrip", label: "Filmstrip" }), toc) %}{% endif %}
{% set hasNoJs = entry.ownScreenshot.noJs and not entry.interstitial %}
{% if hasNoJs %}{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "without-javascript", label: "Without JavaScript" }), toc) %}{% endif %}
{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "lab", label: "Lab metrics" }), toc) %}
{#
	One entry: the history is a subsection of Field data now, and a table of
	contents that lists a heading and its own subheading side by side reads as
	two destinations when it is one.
#}
{% if hasField or hasFieldHistory %}{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "field", label: "Field data" }), toc) %}{% endif %}
{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "weight", label: "Page weight" }), toc) %}
{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "health", label: "Page health" }), toc) %}
{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "hygiene", label: "Hygiene" }), toc) %}
{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "log", label: "Measurement log" }), toc) %}
{% if entry.showEmbed %}{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "embed", label: "Embed" }), toc) %}{% endif %}

<nav class="contents contents-sticky" aria-label="On this page">
	<span class="contents-label">On this page</span>
	<ul>
		{% for item in toc %}
		<li><a href="#{{ item.id }}">{{ item.label }}</a></li>
		{% endfor %}
	</ul>
</nav>

{% if hasStack %}
<section class="panel" id="stack">
	<h2>Stack</h2>
	<div class="stack-list">
		{% if entry.generator %}
		<div class="stack-item">
			{% stackIcon entry.generator, 24 %}
			<span>
				<span class="stack-label block">Built with</span>
				{{ entry.generator.name }}{% if entry.generator.version %} {{ entry.generator.version }}{% endif %}
			</span>
		</div>
		{% endif %}
		{% if entry.host %}
		<div class="stack-item">
			{% stackIcon entry.host, 24 %}
			<span>
				<span class="stack-label block">Hosted by</span>
				{{ entry.host.name }}
			</span>
		</div>
		{% endif %}
	</div>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if hasCwv %}
<section class="panel" id="cwv">
	<h2>
		Core Web Vitals
		{% if entry.cwv.source == "lab" %}
		<span class="tag">lab approximation</span>
		{% else %}
		<span class="tag">field data{% if entry.cwv.period.scope %}, {{ entry.cwv.period.scope }}-level{% endif %}</span>
		{% endif %}
	</h2>

	{% if entry.cwv.source == "lab" %}
	<p class="muted small">
		There is insufficient Chrome UX Report data for this URL, so the numbers shown here are lab approximations.
	</p>
	{% else %}
	<p class="muted small">
		Real Chrome users, 28-day window
		{%- if entry.cwv.period.first %} ({{ entry.cwv.period.first }} → {{ entry.cwv.period.last }}){% endif %}.
		{% if entry.cwv.source == "field-history" %}
		From the backfilled CrUX history rather than this measurement, which predates the API key.
		{% endif %}
	</p>
	{% endif %}
	<div class="cwv">
		{% for part in entry.cwv.parts %}
		<div class="cwv-item cwv-{{ part.rating | ratingClass }}">
			{#
				A value borrowed from the origin is the whole site's number, not
				this page's — CrUX did not have enough samples to report this one
				here. Said out loud rather than left to the reader to assume,
				since the difference matters most for INP, which is the metric
				most often missing.
			#}
			<span class="cwv-key">{{ part.key | upper }}{% if part.proxyFor %} <span class="tag">for {{ part.proxyFor | upper }}</span>{% endif %}{% if part.scope == "origin" %} <span class="tag" title="Not enough samples for this page, so this is the figure for the whole origin">site-wide</span>{% endif %}</span>
			<span class="cwv-value">{{ part.value | unit("ms" if part.key != "cls" else "") }}</span>
			<span class="cwv-rating">{{ part.rating or "no data" }}</span>
		</div>
		{% endfor %}
	</div>
</section>
{% endif %}

<section class="panel" id="scores">
	<h2>Scores</h2>
	<div class="cards">
		{% for score in metrics.SCORES %}
		{% set t = entry.trends[score.key] %}
		{% set value = entry.latest.lab.scores[score.key] %}
		<div class="card">
			<div class="card-value score-{{ value | scoreClass }}">{{ value if value != null else "—" }}</div>
			<div class="card-label">{{ score.label }}</div>
			{% if t %}
			<div class="card-spark">{% sparkline t %}</div>
			{% if t.vsPrevious and not t.vsPrevious.unchanged %}
			<div class="card-delta {{ t.vsPrevious | deltaClass }}">
				{{ t.vsPrevious | deltaArrow }} {{ t.vsPrevious | deltaText }}
			</div>
			{% else %}
			<div class="card-delta flat">no change</div>
			{% endif %}
			{% endif %}
		</div>
		{% endfor %}
	</div>
</section>

{% if hasFilmstrip %}
{#
	The page drawing itself, from the same Lighthouse run the scores above came
	from. Frames are named by a hash of their bytes, so a page that finished
	early and repeated its last frame to the end of the strip stores that frame
	once — the strip still renders at full length from a fraction of the files.

	Horizontally scrollable rather than wrapped: a filmstrip read in two rows is
	no longer a timeline, and the frames are tall and narrow enough that eight of
	them will not fit across a phone.

	Decorative, like the screenshot at the top. The timings under each frame are
	the information here and they are text.
#}
<section class="panel" id="filmstrip">
	<h2>Filmstrip <span class="tag">Lighthouse, {{ entry.latest.formFactor }}</span></h2>
	<p class="muted small">
		How the page drew during the measured load, at
		{{ entry.filmstrip.frames.length }} points across
		{{ entry.filmstrip.frames[entry.filmstrip.frames.length - 1].timing | unit("ms") }}.
	</p>
	<div class="table-scroll">
		<ol class="filmstrip">
			{% for frame in entry.filmstrip.frames %}
			<li class="filmstrip-frame">
				<img src="{{ frame.src }}" width="{{ entry.filmstrip.width }}" height="{{ entry.filmstrip.height }}"
					alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
				<span class="filmstrip-timing">{{ frame.timing | unit("ms") }}</span>
			</li>
			{% endfor %}
		</ol>
	</div>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if hasNoJs %}
{#
	The same page twice: as the browser renders it, and with script execution
	disabled before the navigation. Both are captured on the axe pass, at the
	same viewport, moments apart — so the difference between them is JavaScript
	and nothing else. Neither is a full-page capture, deliberately: a site that
	renders nothing without JavaScript would otherwise produce a tall picture
	beside a short one, and the comparison would be between two aspect ratios.

	Not a claim about whether the site *should* work without JavaScript. Plenty
	of these pages are applications, where the answer is obviously no. It is the
	one thing a screenshot can show that a number cannot.

	Decorative, like the screenshot at the top: the headings above each frame
	carry the information, and they are text.
#}
<section class="panel" id="without-javascript">
	<h2>Without JavaScript</h2>
	<p class="muted small">
		The same load, photographed twice at
		{{ entry.ownScreenshot.width or 800 }}&times;{{ entry.ownScreenshot.height or 600 }}: once with
		scripts disabled, once as rendered.
	</p>
	{#
		The share of pixels that differ between the two captures.

		Described as visual difference and not as "how much of this page needs
		JavaScript", because the two are not the same thing and the gap between
		them is large. A page that renders identically but carries a <noscript>
		banner has its whole layout pushed down by the height of that banner, so
		nearly every pixel moves and the figure lands above 50% — pypi.org is the
		case in point, and it works fine without scripts.

		Not banded green-to-red either: a high number is unremarkable for an
		application and damning for a blog, and this page cannot tell which it is
		looking at. The figure is stated and left alone.
	#}
	{#
		Scripts disabled first, so the eye lands on it before the familiar one.
		The rendered page is what everybody expects a screenshot to be; putting it
		second makes it read as the answer to the picture beside it rather than as
		the subject the no-JS shot is a footnote to.

		The figure comes last, after both, because it is the conclusion drawn from
		the pair rather than a label on either — you read the two, then how far
		apart they are.
	#}
	<div class="nojs-pair">
		{% if entry.clientRendered %}
		{#
			Ahead of the frames, so the reader knows what they are looking at before
			they look. A blank capture beside a full one is ambiguous on sight — it
			could as easily be a load that failed — and the caption under it only
			says "Scripts disabled", not that the emptiness is the finding.

			The same notice treatment the page uses above for stale data and
			redirects, because it is the same kind of statement: a caveat about what
			follows, not a caption on it.
		#}
		<div class="notice notice-warn nojs-verdict">
			<strong><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation csr-icon" width="12" height="12" aria-hidden="true"></i> This page is client rendered.</strong>
			<p>
				Nothing was painted until the scripts ran. This is a web performance and robustness issue!
				{# The capture on the left is
				{{ entry.ownScreenshot.noJsColors }} distinct
				{{ entry.ownScreenshot.noJsColors | plural("color") }}. #}
			</p>
		</div>
		{% endif %}
		<figure class="nojs-shot">
			<figcaption>Scripts disabled</figcaption>
			<img src="{{ entry.ownScreenshot.noJs.src }}"
				width="{{ entry.ownScreenshot.noJs.width or 800 }}" height="{{ entry.ownScreenshot.noJs.height or 600 }}"
				alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
		</figure>
		<figure class="nojs-shot">
			<figcaption>As rendered</figcaption>
			<img src="{{ entry.ownScreenshot.src }}"
				width="{{ entry.ownScreenshot.width or 800 }}" height="{{ entry.ownScreenshot.height or 600 }}"
				alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
		</figure>
		{% if entry.ownScreenshot.difference !== null %}
		<p class="nojs-diff">
			<strong>{{ entry.ownScreenshot.difference }}%</strong>
			<span class="muted small">of pixels differ &mdash;
				{% if entry.ownScreenshot.difference >= 95 %}they have almost nothing in common
				{%- elif entry.ownScreenshot.difference >= 50 %}the page changes substantially
				{%- elif entry.ownScreenshot.difference >= 10 %}parts of the page change
				{%- elif entry.ownScreenshot.difference > 0 %}they are nearly identical
				{%- else %}they are identical{% endif %}.
				A shifted layout counts as difference, so a &lt;noscript&gt; banner alone
				can account for a large figure.</span>
		</p>
		{% endif %}
	</div>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% macro trendtable(rows, entryData, prefix="") %}
<div class="table-scroll">
<table class="grid">
	<thead>
		<tr>
			<th class="col-site">Metric</th>
			<th>Current</th>
			<th>Previous</th>
			<th>Change</th>
			<th>Best</th>
			<th>Worst</th>
			<th class="col-trend">Trend</th>
		</tr>
	</thead>
	<tbody>
	{% for row in rows %}
	{% set t = entryData.trends[prefix + row.key] %}
	{% if t %}
		<tr>
			<td class="col-site">
				<strong>{{ row.label }}</strong>
				{% if row.note %}<span class="muted small block">{{ row.note }}</span>{% endif %}
			</td>
			<td class="num">{{ t.current | unit(row.unit) }}</td>
			<td class="num muted">{{ t.previous | unit(row.unit) }}</td>
			<td class="num {{ t.vsPrevious | deltaClass }}">
				{% if t.vsPrevious %}{{ t.vsPrevious | deltaArrow }} {{ t.vsPrevious | deltaText(row.unit) }}{% else %}—{% endif %}
				{% if t.significant %}<span class="badge badge-sig" title="Larger than this metric's normal run-to-run noise">significant</span>{% endif %}
			</td>
			<td class="num muted">{{ (t.min if t.lowerIsBetter else t.max) | unit(row.unit) }}</td>
			<td class="num muted">{{ (t.max if t.lowerIsBetter else t.min) | unit(row.unit) }}</td>
			<td class="col-trend">{% sparkline t %}</td>
		</tr>
	{% endif %}
	{% endfor %}
	</tbody>
</table>
</div>
{% endmacro %}

<section class="panel" id="lab">
	<h2>Lab metrics <span class="tag">Lighthouse, {{ entry.latest.formFactor }}</span></h2>
	{{ trendtable(metrics.LAB_METRICS, entry) }}

	{% if entry.latest.lab.lcpBreakdown %}
	<h3>LCP Subparts <span class="tag">lab observed, unthrottled</span></h3>
	<p class="muted small">
		The subparts attribute LCP to your server, resource discovery, download, or rendering. Note that the Lab metrics LCP metric shown above <em>is</em> throttled.
	</p>
	{#
		These will not add up to the LCP above, and the gap is usually large — 225
		milliseconds against 4.07 seconds on one site here. Both are honest; they
		are measured in different worlds.

		Lighthouse runs with `throttlingMethod: "simulate"`: it loads the page at
		this machine's real speed, records that trace, and then models what the
		load would have taken on an emulated phone over slow 4G. LCP above is the
		model's answer. These subparts are read off the observed trace, before any
		of that modelling. The same record shows the difference plainly — the
		server answered in 44ms, and the model bills 620ms for it.

		The ratio is not a constant to divide out: it depends on the request
		graph, and runs from about 10x to 20x across this corpus. So the durations
		are shown as measured and labeled for what they are, rather than scaled
		to a total they were never part of.
	#}
	<div class="breakdown">
		{% set lcpMax = entry.latest.lab.lcpBreakdown | sortByValue | maxValue("value") %}
		{% for part in entry.latest.lab.lcpBreakdown | sortByValue %}
		<div class="breakdown-row">
			<span class="breakdown-label breakdown-key">{{ part.key | humanize }}</span>
			{% bar part.value, lcpMax, "warn" %}
			<span class="breakdown-value">{{ part.value | ms }}</span>
		</div>
		{% endfor %}
	</div>
	{% endif %}
</section>

{#
	Either half is enough to earn the section: a site CrUX has stopped reporting
	still has the weeks it did report, and that history is the whole reason to
	come here. Each half is gated separately below.
#}
{% if hasField or hasFieldHistory %}
<section class="panel" id="field">
	<h2>
		Field data
		{%- if hasField %} <span class="tag">Chrome UX Report, {{ entry.latest.field.scope }}-level</span>{% endif %}
	</h2>
	{% if hasField %}
	<p class="muted small">
		Real Chrome users, 28-day trailing window
		({{ entry.latest.field.collectionPeriod.first }} → {{ entry.latest.field.collectionPeriod.last }}).
	</p>

	<div class="table-scroll">
	<table class="grid">
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th class="col-site">Metric</th>
				<th>p75</th>
				<th>Rating</th>
				<th class="col-dist">Distribution (good / needs work / poor)</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
		{% for row in metrics.FIELD_METRICS %}
		{% set m = entry.latest.field.metrics[row.key] %}
		{% if m %}
			<tr>
				<td class="col-site">
					<strong>{{ row.label }}</strong>
					{% if row.coreWebVital %}<span class="badge badge-cwv">CWV</span>{% endif %}
					{% if row.note %}<span class="muted small block">{{ row.note }}</span>{% endif %}
				</td>
				<td class="num">{{ m.p75 | unit(row.unit) }}</td>
				<td><span class="pill pill-{{ m.rating | ratingClass }}">{{ m.rating or "—" }}</span></td>
				<td class="col-dist">
					{% if m.distribution %}
					<span class="dist" title="{{ m.distribution.good }}% good, {{ m.distribution.needsImprovement }}% needs improvement, {{ m.distribution.poor }}% poor">
						<span class="dist-good" style="width:{{ m.distribution.good }}%"></span>
						<span class="dist-avg" style="width:{{ m.distribution.needsImprovement }}%"></span>
						<span class="dist-poor" style="width:{{ m.distribution.poor }}%"></span>
					</span>
					<span class="muted small">{{ m.distribution.good }}% good</span>
					{% else %}—{% endif %}
				</td>
			</tr>
		{% endif %}
		{% endfor %}
		</tbody>
	</table>
	</div>

	{% if entry.labVsField %}
	<h3>Lab LCP vs Field LCP</h3>
	{#
		Two bars against one scale, so the comparison is a length rather than a
		subtraction the reader performs. Both are widths of the larger of the two,
		which is the only scale on which "twice as long" looks twice as long.

		The lab bar is neutral and the field bar carries its Core Web Vitals
		rating: the lab number has no verdict attached — it is a simulation — while
		the field number is the one Google grades.
	#}
	{% set lvfMax = entry.labVsField.lab if entry.labVsField.lab > entry.labVsField.field else entry.labVsField.field %}
	{#
		Recomputed from the two stored millisecond figures rather than read from
		`labVsField.ratio`, which is rounded to a tenth for the leaderboards — a
		column of a thousand rows wants a figure you can scan, and this is one
		site, where 1.17 says more than 1.2. Both numbers it is derived from are on
		screen directly above, so the arithmetic is checkable.
	#}
	{% set lvfRatio = entry.labVsField.lab / entry.labVsField.field %}
	<div class="lvf-compare">
		<div class="lvf-row">
			<span class="lvf-name">Lab</span>
			<span class="bar"><span class="bar-fill lvf-fill-lab" style="width: {{ (entry.labVsField.lab / lvfMax * 100) | round }}%"></span></span>
			<span class="lvf-ms">{{ entry.labVsField.lab | ms }}</span>
		</div>
		<div class="lvf-row">
			<span class="lvf-name">Field p75</span>
			<span class="bar"><span class="bar-fill lvf-fill-{{ entry.latest.field.metrics.lcp.rating | ratingClass }}" style="width: {{ (entry.labVsField.field / lvfMax * 100) | round }}%"></span></span>
			<span class="lvf-ms">{{ entry.labVsField.field | ms }}</span>
		</div>
	</div>
	<p class="muted small">
		{#
			The multiple, then what it implies — rather than "N× faster", which only
			reads correctly in one direction: a field number worse than the lab
			gives a figure below 1, and "0.33× slower" is not a sentence.

			And not "faster than the test says", which sounds like the test was
			wrong. It was not: it measured a mid-range phone on a throttled
			connection, deliberately. A field number better than that says something
			about who is visiting, not about the accuracy of the lab — hence
			"likely", since this is an inference about an audience rather than a
			measurement of one.
		#}
		The lab LCP is <strong>{{ lvfRatio | round(2) }}×</strong> the field p75 —
		{#
			Within a tenth either way is not a finding. `fieldWorse` is a strict
			comparison, so without this band a site a single millisecond apart would
			be told its audience is below average.

			Tested on the rounded ratio, which is the one displayed: a reader seeing
			0.9x should get the same verdict as anyone else seeing 0.9x, rather than
			two sites reading identically and being described differently.
		#}
		{% if lvfRatio >= 0.9 and lvfRatio <= 1.1 %}
		real users likely have similar hardware or network connectivity to the lab test.
		{%- else %}
		real users likely have {{ "below" if entry.labVsField.fieldWorse else "above" }}-average
		hardware or network connectivity.
		{%- endif %}
		{#
			1.5x worse or more, expressed the way round the ratio now runs: 1/1.5
			is 0.667, which one decimal renders as 0.7 — so the test has to include
			it rather than sit below a value the data can no longer produce.
		#}
		{% if entry.labVsField.fieldWorse and lvfRatio <= 0.7 %}
		A gap this wide usually means real users are on slower devices or networks
		than the simulated ones — the lab number is flattering you.
		{% endif %}
	</p>
	{% endif %}
	{% endif %}

	{% if hasFieldHistory %}
	<h3 id="field-history">Field history <span class="tag">{{ entry.fieldHistory.series.length }} weeks</span></h3>
	<p class="muted small">
		Weekly p75 from the CrUX History API, backfilled in a single request.
	</p>
	<div class="table-scroll">
	<table class="grid compact">
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th class="col-site">Week ending</th>
				<th>LCP</th><th>INP</th><th>CLS</th><th>FCP</th><th>TTFB</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
		{% for point in entry.fieldHistory.series | reverse | limit(12) %}
			<tr>
				<td class="col-site muted">{{ point.date }}</td>
				{% for key in ["lcp", "inp", "cls", "fcp", "ttfb"] %}
				{% set m = point.metrics[key] %}
				<td class="num"><span class="pill pill-{{ m.rating | ratingClass }}">{{ m.p75 | unit("" if key == "cls" else "ms") }}</span></td>
				{% endfor %}
			</tr>
		{% endfor %}
		</tbody>
	</table>
	</div>
	{% endif %}
</section>
{% endif %}

<section class="panel" id="weight">
	<h2>Page weight</h2>
	{{ trendtable(metrics.WEIGHT_METRICS, entry) }}

	{% if entry.weightByType.length %}
	<h3>By resource type</h3>
	{#
		One bar rather than a row per type. The question here is what the page is
		made of, and that is a question about proportions — a stack answers it in
		one line, where six bars scaled to the largest of them made the reader do
		the dividing.

		Shares come from lib/report.js, which also drops `third-party`: it is a
		tally across the other types rather than one of them, and adding it here
		would take the bar past its own total.
	#}
	<div class="stackbar" role="img"
		aria-label="Page weight by resource type{% for t in entry.weightByType %}. {{ t.key | humanize }} {{ t.shareText }}, {{ t.bytes | bytes }} over {{ t.requests }} {{ t.requests | plural("request") }}{% endfor %}.">
		{%- for t in entry.weightByType %}
		<span class="stackbar-part type-{{ t.key }}" style="width: {{ t.share }}%"
			title="{{ t.key | humanize }} — {{ t.bytes | bytes }} ({{ t.shareText }}) over {{ t.requests }} {{ t.requests | plural('request') }}"></span>
		{%- endfor %}
	</div>

	{#
		The legend carries the numbers the bar cannot. Ordered as the bar is, so
		reading down the list walks left to right along it.
	#}
	<ul class="stackkey">
		{%- for t in entry.weightByType %}
		<li>
			<span class="stackkey-swatch type-{{ t.key }}"></span>
			<span class="stackkey-name">{{ t.key | humanize }}</span>
			<span class="stackkey-share">{{ t.shareText }}</span>
			<span class="stackkey-value muted">{{ t.bytes | bytes }} · {{ t.requests }} req</span>
		</li>
		{%- endfor %}
	</ul>
	{% endif %}

	{% if entry.latest.lab.thirdParty and entry.latest.lab.thirdParty.count %}
	<h3>Third parties <span class="tag">{{ entry.latest.lab.thirdParty.count }} entities</span></h3>
	<p class="muted small">
		{{ entry.latest.lab.thirdParty.bytes | bytes }} transferred,
		{{ entry.latest.lab.thirdParty.mainThreadMs | ms }} on the main thread.
	</p>
	<div class="breakdown">
		{% set tpMax = entry.latest.lab.thirdParty.top | maxValue("bytes") %}
		{% for tp in entry.latest.lab.thirdParty.top %}
		<div class="breakdown-row">
			<span class="breakdown-label">{{ tp.entity }}</span>
			{% bar tp.bytes, tpMax, "warn" %}
			<span class="breakdown-value">{{ tp.bytes | bytes }} <span class="muted">· {{ tp.mainThreadMs | ms }} CPU</span></span>
		</div>
		{% endfor %}
	</div>
	{% endif %}
</section>

<section class="panel" id="health">
	<h2>Page health</h2>
	{{ trendtable(metrics.HEALTH_METRICS, entry) }}

	{% if entry.latest.lab.mainThread and entry.latest.lab.mainThread.byGroup %}
	<h3>Main thread work</h3>
	<div class="breakdown">
		{% set mtMax = entry.latest.lab.mainThread.byGroup | sortByValue | maxValue("value") %}
		{% for part in entry.latest.lab.mainThread.byGroup | sortByValue %}
		<div class="breakdown-row">
			<span class="breakdown-label breakdown-key">{{ part.key | humanize }}</span>
			{% bar part.value, mtMax, "neutral" %}
			<span class="breakdown-value">{{ part.value | ms }}</span>
		</div>
		{% endfor %}
	</div>
	{% endif %}

	{% if entry.axe %}
	<h3>
		Axe violations
		<span class="tag">axe-core {{ entry.axe.version }}, full rule set</span>
	</h3>
	{% if entry.axe.error %}
	<p class="muted small">The axe run failed: <code>{{ entry.axe.error }}</code></p>
	{% else %}
	<p class="muted small">
		<strong>{{ entry.axe.violations }}</strong> violating node{{ "s" if entry.axe.violations != 1 }}
		across {{ entry.axe.violationRules }} rule{{ "s" if entry.axe.violationRules != 1 }},
		{{ entry.axe.passes }} passing. This is a standalone Axe CLI run and is stricter than the
		subset Lighthouse already includes.
	</p>
	{% if entry.axe.top.length %}
	<ul class="failures">
		{% for v in entry.axe.top %}
		<li>
			<span class="badge badge-{{ 'error' if v.impact == 'critical' or v.impact == 'serious' else 'stale' }}">{{ v.impact }}</span>
			<code>{{ v.id }}</code> — {{ v.help }}
			<span class="badge">{{ v.nodes }} node{{ "s" if v.nodes != 1 }}</span>
		</li>
		{% endfor %}
	</ul>
	{% endif %}
	{% endif %}
	{% endif %}

	{% if entry.latest.lab.accessibility.failing.length %}
	<h3>Accessibility failures <span class="tag">{{ entry.latest.lab.accessibility.failingCount }} of {{ entry.latest.lab.accessibility.applicableCount }} audits</span></h3>
	<ul class="failures">
		{% for fail in entry.latest.lab.accessibility.failing %}
		<li>
			<code>{{ fail.id }}</code> — {{ fail.title }}
			{% if fail.nodes %}<span class="badge">{{ fail.nodes }} node{{ "s" if fail.nodes != 1 }}</span>{% endif %}
		</li>
		{% endfor %}
	</ul>
	{% endif %}
</section>

<section class="panel" id="hygiene">
	<h2>Hygiene</h2>
	<div class="chips">
		{% set h = entry.latest.lab.hygiene %}
		{% for check in [
			{k: "https", label: "HTTPS"},
			{k: "hsts", label: "HSTS"},
			{k: "csp", label: "CSP"},
			{k: "clickjacking", label: "Clickjacking protection"},
			{k: "originIsolation", label: "Origin isolation"},
			{k: "trustedTypes", label: "Trusted Types"},
			{k: "bfCache", label: "bfcache"}
		] %}
		<span class="chip chip-{{ 'good' if h[check.k] == 1 else ('poor' if h[check.k] == 0 else 'none') }}">
			{{ check.label }}
		</span>
		{% endfor %}
		<span class="chip chip-none">{{ h.protocol or "—" }}</span>
		{% if h.consoleErrors %}<span class="chip chip-poor">{{ h.consoleErrors }} console error{{ "s" if h.consoleErrors != 1 }}</span>{% endif %}
	</div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="log">
	<h2>Measurement log</h2>
	<p class="muted small">
		Every stored run for this URL, newest first.
	</p>
	<div class="table-scroll">
	<table class="grid compact">
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th class="col-site">When</th>
				<th>Perf</th><th>LCP</th><th>CLS</th><th>TBT</th><th>Weight</th>
				<th>Runs</th><th>Spread</th><th>Bench</th><th>Took</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
		{% for point in entry.history | reverse | limit(30) %}
			{#
				A generator change is a fact about this row: the measurement below
				is the first one that saw the new tag. Rendered as its own row
				above it rather than a badge inside it, so the change reads as
				something that happened between two measurements — which is all we
				can honestly say about when it happened.
			#}
			{% for change in entry.generatorHistory %}
			{% if change.date == point.date %}
			<tr class="row-change">
				<td colspan="11">
					<span class="change-mark">{{ "↩" if change.returnedToEleventy else "→" }}</span>
					Generator changed from <strong>{{ change.from }}</strong> to
					<strong>{{ change.to }}</strong>
					{% if change.leftEleventy %}<span class="badge badge-redirect" title="This is the move 11ty Emeritus is for — measurement follows it on its own">left Eleventy</span>{% endif %}
					{% if change.returnedToEleventy %}<span class="badge badge-unlisted" title="Back on Eleventy or Build Awesome — it returns to Built Awesome on its own">back on Eleventy</span>{% endif %}
					<span class="muted small">first seen this measurement</span>
				</td>
			</tr>
			{% endif %}
			{% endfor %}
			<tr{% if point.error %} class="row-failing"{% endif %}>
				<td class="col-site">
					{{ point.date | date("long") }}
					{% if entry.movedFrom and point.via != entry.url %}
					<span class="badge" title="Measured at {{ point.via }}, before the move">old URL</span>
					{% endif %}
				</td>
				{% if point.error %}
					<td colspan="8" class="muted"><code>{{ point.error }}</code></td>
					<td class="num muted">{{ (point.durationMs / 1000) | round(1) }}s</td>
				{% else %}
					<td class="score score-{{ point.performance | scoreClass }}">{{ point.performance }}</td>
					<td class="num">{{ point.lcp | ms }}</td>
					<td class="num">{{ point.cls | unit("") }}</td>
					<td class="num">{{ point.tbt | ms }}</td>
					<td class="num">{{ point.total | bytes }}</td>
					<td class="num muted">{{ point.completedRuns }}/{{ point.requestedRuns }}</td>
					<td class="num muted">{{ ("±" + point.spread) if point.spread != null else "—" }}</td>
					{#
						The benchmark index, and how far off the project median it
						sat. On its own the number means nothing to a reader — 2470 is
						not good or bad — but "+27%" explains a TBT that jumped
						without the site changing, which is the only reason this
						column is here.

						Against the project median rather than the row above, so each
						row stands alone: the step between two rows says nothing about
						whether either machine was a fair one to measure on. Marked
						when it falls outside the same band the notice at the top of
						the page draws, so a reader can see which rows that notice is
						about.
					#}
					<td class="num muted" title="Lighthouse benchmark index of the measuring machine{% if point.benchVsMedian != null %} — {{ point.benchVsMedian | signed }}% against this project's median{% if point.benchOutside %}, outside the {{ sites.benchmark.driftBand.pct }}% band{% endif %}{% endif %}">
						{{ point.bench | num }}
						{%- if point.benchVsMedian != null %}
						<span class="bench-change {{ 'outside' if point.benchOutside }}">{{ point.benchVsMedian | signed }}%</span>
						{%- endif %}
					</td>
					<td class="num muted">{{ (point.durationMs / 1000) | round(1) }}s</td>
				{% endif %}
			</tr>
		{% endfor %}
		</tbody>
	</table>
	</div>
</section>

{% endif %}

{#
	Only where a category asks for it — see `showEmbed` in config/sites.js. The
	table of contents entry above is behind the same condition, so a page without
	the section does not advertise one.
#}
{% if entry.showEmbed %}
<section class="panel" id="embed">
	<h2>Embed this score</h2>
	<p class="muted small">
		Use <a href="/embed/">this web component to show {{ entry.displayUrl }}'s scores on any website</a>, updated whenever this instance re-measures. <em>Don’t forget to bundle the <code>speedlify2-score.js</code> component code into your site!</em>
	</p>
	{#
		The live one and the snippet resolve their instance URL differently, and
		the difference is the point.

		This example runs on this site, so htmlBaseUrl — the same base every other
		link here uses, correct whether served from a subdirectory or a root.

		The snippet below gets pasted onto a *different* site, where a path like
		/speedlify2/ would resolve against that site's domain. It needs the
		absolute URL, which is meta.url.
	#}
	<script type="module" src="/js/speedlify2-score.js"></script>
	<p class="embed-live">
		<speedlify2-score speedlify-url="{{ '/' | htmlBaseUrl }}" url="{{ entry.url }}"></speedlify2-score>
	</p>

	<pre><code>&lt;speedlify2-score speedlify-url="{{ meta.url }}/" url="{{ entry.url }}"&gt;&lt;/speedlify2-score&gt;</code></pre>
</section>
{% endif %}

</article>
